The '''Camboriú War''' ({{Langx|bl|Guerra de Camboriú}}) was a three-month war between the [[Baliscan Confederation]] and the [[Potocsí|Republic of Potocsí]]. The war was a part of the larger [[Balisca-Potocsí conflict]], related to their disputes over the Baliscan-controlled territories in the [[Marañón basin]]. It followed a period of escalation in the form of troop mobilizations and violations of land borders and airspace by both parties. It was the first major armed conflict to occur in [[Abyala]] since the [[Abyalan Campaign]] (193x) of the [[Great Adonian War]] (193x-1948).

The conflict has had a strong effect in both countries and has been the subject of various books, articles, films, and songs. Patriotic sentiment ran high in Potocsí thanks to it's initial successes in the war, but the unfavorable outcome prompted large protests against the ruling government. In Balisca, the government of [[W:Trotskyism|Trotskyist]]–[[W:Posadist|Posadist]] [[Mário Sá Justino]] was bolstered by the successful performance of the [[Confederal Armed Forces|Armed Forces]] and their hardline stance against a diplomatic negotiation with the Potocsino government. The cultural, military and political effect of the conflict has been more visible in Balisca than in Potocsí, influencing the diplomatic reorientation of the country towards [[Burawa]] and the [[Socialist Bloc]] and the deterioration of [[Balisca-Breisland relations|relations with Breisland]] and [[Balisca-Volisania relations|Volisania]], alongside a substantial buildup of the [[Confederal Armed Forces]].

Diplomatic relations between Balisca and Potocsí were restored in 1984 following years of negotiation fostered by the governments of [[Asikyira]] and [[Tapuya]], at which the two governments issued a joint statement on normalization. No change in either country's position regarding the status of the [[Marañón basin]] was made explicit until the signing of the 1982 [[Kuluba Accords]].

==Preclude==
{{Main|Potocsino-Baliscan conflict}}

=== Failed diplomacy ===
Balisca had long been locked in territorial disputes with Potocsí, with the latter rejecting Baliscan sovereignty over the [[Estuarian wetlands]]. The [[Northern Confederation]] had asserted control over the region during the collapse of Volisanian authority during the [[Volisanian Civil War]]. The dispute was compounded by the earlier Potocsino occupation of southern Baliscan territory during the [[Baliscan Revolution]] (1918–1921), when Potocsino forces established the puppet [[Republic of Meridiana]] in the Estuarian wetlands — a territory that was [[Liberation of Meridiana|re-annexed into Balisca]] at the start of the [[Great Adonian War]]. The memory of Meridiana reinforced Baliscan suspicion of Potocsino territorial ambitions and shaped the strategic calculus of both governments for decades.

By 1973, the exploitation of large oil and natural gas deposits near the [[Garrigàs Lake|Garrigàs Gulf]] by the state-owned [[Baliscan Oil Corporation]] (COBAL) had led to heightened tensions between the two countries, with Potocsí mobilizing its forces on the disputed border. This was a significant escalation of long-standing tensions between the two countries, and surprised the Baliscan military, which had a minimal military presence in the territory in line with its treaties with [[Indigenous nations in Balisca|Indigenous nations]]. Potocsí claimed sovereignty over portions of the Marañón basin, particularly in the [[Volisanian language|Volisanian-speaking]] [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]], stemming back to the colonial era.

A series of diplomatic initiatives between 1973 and 1976 failed to resolve the dispute. The [[Adonian Community]] offered mediation, which Potocsí rejected, insisting on bilateral negotiations that Balisca viewed as a delaying tactic while Potocsino forces continued to mass along the frontier. By mid-1976, both countries had deployed significant forces to the border region, and a series of clashes along the border further inflamed tensions.

===Potoscino military junta===
{{Main|Military dictatorship in Potocsí}}
The Potocsino government of [[Jose Luis Ongania]] had come to power through a [[1958 Potocsino coup d'état|military coup in 1958]], establishing a right-wing military junta that pursued an aggressive nationalist agenda. The Ongania regime promoted the concept of [[Recuperació Nacional|''Recuperació Nacional'']] ("National Recovery"), which framed the [[Estuarian wetlands]] as rightfully Potocsino territory occupied by [[Baliscan expansionism]]. The regime's propaganda emphasized the oil wealth of the region, and linked Potocsí's economic situation with access to the [[Garrigàs Gulf|Garrigàs Gulf's]] resources.

The junta's internal position was increasingly precarious by 1976. Economic stagnation, labor unrest, and the suppression of political opposition had eroded the regime's popular support. A successful war to "recover" the Marañón was seen by Ongania and his inner circle — particularly Generals [[Justo Guillermo Padilla]] and [[Andrés Manuel Ortiz]] — as a means of rallying domestic support and legitimizing the military government. The calculation was that a swift, decisive victory would be achievable before the Baliscan military could mobilize, given Balisca's policy of armed neutrality and its minimal military footprint in the the lower Marañón basin.

=== Baliscan domestic crisis ===
{{Main|October 76}}
Under the Baliscan constitution, confederal elections were scheduled for October 1976. However, in March 1976, Secretary General [[Mário Sá Justino]] — citing escalating tensions with Potocsí along the disputed border regions and a series of armed skirmishes in the lower [[Marañón basin|Marañón]] — announced the postponement of elections for one year. The decision was made under the provisions of a [[confederal emergency]] declared in February 1976 following the intensification of [[W:Territorial dispute|border clashes]] and Potocsino military mobilization along [[Balisca-Potocsí border|the border]].

The postponement proved deeply controversial. While the [[Revolutionary Front]] (FR) argued that the security situation required stable governance, critics — including figures within the FR's own coalition — contended that the postponement represented an unconstitutional power grab and a betrayal of democratic principles. The [[Confederal Congress]] had not been consulted on the decision, and several FR delegates publicly broke with Justino over the issue. The resulting unrest, began as a series of anti-authoritarian student occupation protests and expanded in scope, rallying against increasing [[W:Elitism|elitism]] in traditional institutions, rising income disparities between urban and [[Rural society in Balisca|rural society]], and the Revolutionary Front's embrace of "[[W:Socialism from above|socialism from above]]." Protests intensified after Justino forcefully condemned the demonstrations as "unpatriotic" at a time of increased tensions with Potocsí, and called for the breaking up of the protests as part of the 1976 confederal emergency declaration. This led [[Trade unions in Balisca|Balisca's trade union confederations]] to call for [[W:Sympathy strikes|sympathy strikes]], which spread far more quickly than expected to involve 14 million workers — more than 16% of the total population — and the movement came to be characterized by spontaneous and decentralized wildcat disposition.

The domestic crisis drove a wedge between the civilian population, the [[Autonomous Militia]], and the [[Confederal Armed Forces]], providing an opportunity for Potocsino forces to launch their invasion. Street clashes between protestors (supported by autonomous militias) and the [[Carabineros de Balisca|''Carabineros'']] complicated the confederal response to the aggression. On 28 December, the Confederal Congress — including the plurality of the Revolutionary Front — voted to censure Justino and ordered the Carabineros to stand down, further disrupting the chain of command during a critical phase of the war.

