==Baliscan counter-offensive and strategy==

=== Baliscan support for Indigenous separatists ===
{{Main|Internal conflict in Potocsí}}The Baliscan counteroffensive was significantly augmented by support for Indigenous separatist movements within Potocsí itself. The Potocsino government had long suppressed its Indigenous populations, particularly the [[Kawsani people|Kawsani]] and [[Jachani people|Jachani]] peoples of the western highlands, who had been subjected to forced assimilation, land dispossession, and military repression. The Baliscan intelligence services, operating through contacts with the [[Baliscan Mafia]] and the [[International Brigades]], established contact with Indigenous militants and provided them with arms, training, and coordination.

The intensification of the Indigenous uprising in Potocsí's western highlands forced the Ongania regime to divert significant forces from the Marañón front, weakening the Potocsino position at a critical moment. The internal conflict also undermined the regime's domestic legitimacy, as the war that was supposed to rally the nation instead exposed the depth of internal fractures.

=== Potocsino scorched earth policy ===
{{Main|Marañón oil fires}}
As Potocsino forces retreated from the Marañón basin in late November 1976, they implemented a systematic [[W:Scorched earth policy|scorched earth policy]]. The order to destroy the [[Petroleum industry in Balisca|petroleum infrastructure]] came directly from Potocsino General [[Ramón Velázquez]], who had been instructed to "deny the enemy the resources to continue their war effort." Potocsino combat engineers used explosive charges to rupture wellheads and ignite the escaping crude oil, targeting the most productive wells first and working through the fields methodically as they withdrew.

The resulting [[Marañón oil fires]] — approximately 612 oil wells, 105 storage tanks, and 6 major facilities set ablaze across the basin — constituted one of the worst environmental disasters in Abyalan history. The fires burned from 23 November 1976 until the final well was extinguished on 15 April 1977, releasing massive quantities of crude oil, smoke, and atmospheric pollutants into the surrounding environment. The resulting [[W:Black rain|petroleum rain]] and smoke plumes contaminated vast areas of the [[Marañón rainforest]], causing severe health impacts for Indigenous populations in the region.

